APSI's changes to the vendored FourQlib sources.

The files are otherwise pristine upstream, so re-pinning is: copy the new sources from
FourQ_64bit_and_portable/ over this directory, then apply this patch. Reversing it reproduces the
upstream sources exactly, which is the check to run afterwards:

    git apply --check --reverse third_party/fourq/apsi-changes.patch

Nine changes, in descending order of how much they matter.

  * AMD64/fp_x64.h and ARM64/fp_arm64.h move field elements between felm_t and uint128_t with
    memcpy rather than through a cast. A field element is an array of digit_t, which neither
    aliases a uint128_t nor carries its alignment, so mod1271, fpadd1271, fpsub1271 and
    fpneg1271 must not read or write one through a uint128_t lvalue. Every GCC or Clang build
    that selects one of these two architecture headers compiles this, which is Linux and macOS
    as well as MinGW; assembly support does not replace these four routines. MSVC takes the
    intrinsic path instead, and a generic build uses generic/fp.h, which is free of the
    pattern. The <string.h> this needs is included outside the extern "C" block, since these
    headers are included from C++ and a standard header must not be given C language linkage.

  * eccp2_core.c, generic/fp.h and AMD64/fp_x64.h drop the __inline qualifier from 31
    definitions. Twenty are non-static and called from other translation units, and MSVC emits
    no external symbol for such a definition, so those callers need the qualifier gone to link.
    The Unix toolchains emit the symbol either way.

  * FourQ_internal.h's generic MUL128, ADD128 and ADC128 stage through digit_t buffers rather
    than casting to them. They hand mp_mul and mp_add, which take digit_t, operands that are
    uint64_t and a generic uint128_t that is an array of uint64_t, and on the 32-bit targets
    digit_t is not uint64_t. The sizes agree either way, so the operands keep their layout:
    NWORDS_FIELD digits span 16 bytes whether a digit is 64 or 32 bits. The staging buffers are
    not covered by the optional TEMP_ZEROING scrubbing, which is off by default.

  * AMD64/fp2_1271_AVX2.S drops its #include of consts.s. Upstream generates that file from
    consts.c as a build step; APSI compiles consts.c as an ordinary source, so the constants
    are linked in rather than textually included.

  * Both AMD64/*.S files carry a .note.GNU-stack section. GNU ld marks the stack of the whole
    output executable when any input object lacks one, so an ELF link that draws either object
    out of the archive needs it to keep NX. The linker warns on each such link, and the
    deprecation it mentions is of the inference itself, so a future version stops guessing
    rather than starts refusing. Upstream omits the section.

  * FourQ_internal.h selects the 128-bit arithmetic by compiler rather than by operating
    system, and selects the matching digit macros off the support macro that choice sets rather
    than by restating the target matrix. The distinction the code needs is the compiler: GCC
    and Clang have a native 128-bit integer on the AMD64 and ARM64 targets these branches
    select, and MSVC has none. MinGW therefore takes the same UINT128_SUPPORT path as AMD64
    Linux, and AMD64/fp_x64.h and eccp2.c both switch on that macro rather than on the target.
    ARM64 stays paired with Linux; APSI's CMake compiles ARM64 Windows as a generic build,
    which does not reach these branches.

  * Every #include of a header in this same directory carries an "apsi/fourq/" prefix, so that
    it resolves through the mirrored build-tree layout described in README.md. The "../"
    includes in the architecture headers already resolve through that layout and are left
    alone.

  * crypto_util.c reduces both x-coordinate components in decode before the sign bit is read,
    as FourQlib's 32-bit implementation does. The only value this affects is the redundant
    encoding of zero, where a component holds p rather than 0.

  * FourQ_internal.h gives a generic build the native 128-bit integer when the compiler has
    one, and eccp2.c's mul_truncate excludes that case from the branch that indexes
    uint128_t as a pair of digits. Microsoft SEAL names the same native type uint128_t in
    the global namespace, so without this a generic build cannot compile any translation
    unit that includes both, which is why ecpoint.cpp failed under GCC and Clang. MSVC
    defines no __SIZEOF_INT128__ and SEAL's MSVC header declares no uint128_t, so the
    ARM64 Windows build keeps the pair of digits and the branch it already took.

To regenerate: check out the pinned commit, copy this tree and the upstream tree into two
directories that share the third_party/fourq/ prefix, and diff them with git diff
--no-index, covering exactly the files listed above. Keep this header and keep each
hunk's line endings as the file itself has them; upstream stores hash_to_curve.c with LF
and the rest with CRLF, so a patch normalised to one ending will not apply. README.md and
the CMakeLists.txt files are APSI's own and are deliberately absent from the patch, so
restore them after copying upstream over this directory.
